RFC 5545: Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification
RFC 5545 is the IETF standard that defines the iCalendar data format for representing and exchanging calendaring and scheduling information such as events, to-dos, journal entries, and free/busy data. Published in September 2009, it’s a Standards Track document that obsoletes the earlier RFC 2445, updating and clarifying the core object model for calendar interoperability.

Key Features and Structure
The top-level object is the VCALENDAR component, which begins with BEGIN:VCALENDAR and ends with END:VCALENDAR.

Within VCALENDAR, calendar properties like PRODID and VERSION apply to the entire calendar.

Calendar components include:

VEVENT for events with start/end timestamps (DTSTART, DTEND).

VTODO for to-do items with due dates.

VJOURNAL for journal entries that carry descriptive text.

VFREEBUSY for publishing or requesting free/busy information.

VTIMEZONE for time zone definitions and daylight-savings rules.

VALARM for alarms attached to other components.

Properties and value types conform to grammar rules in Sections 2–3, using UTF-8 encoding, CRLF line endings, and continuations for long lines.

Historical Context and Adoption
RFC 5545 grew out of the earlier vCalendar format (RFC 2445) and was created by the IETF’s Calendaring and Scheduling Working Group. By superseding RFC 2445, it standardized a single, vendor-neutral interchange format under the media type text/calendar and the file extensions .ics and .ifb.

Major calendar systems—Apple Calendar, Google Calendar, Microsoft Outlook, and many open-source clients like Thunderbird/Lightning—read and write iCalendar data. The widespread support has made RFC 5545 the cornerstone of cross-platform scheduling and sharing.

Relationship to Other Standards
iTIP (RFC 2446) defines the scheduling methods (e.g., PUBLISH, REQUEST, REPLY) for exchanging iCalendar objects between users.

iMIP (RFC 2447) specifies how to send iTIP messages over email (SMTP/IMAP).

CalDAV (RFC 4791) builds on iCalendar to provide WebDAV-based calendar access and manipulation.

Extensions such as RFC 5546 (iCalendar grouping), RFC 7986 (additional components), and RFC 6868 (parameter encoding) further enhance interoperability.

Practical Implementation Tips
In Python, libraries like icalendar or ics.py parse and generate RFC 5545 data structures.

For recurrence rules (RRULE), use libraries such as dateutil.rrule to construct or interpret repeating events per Section 3.3.10. 

To serve calendars over the web, set Content-Type: text/calendar; charset=utf-8 and stream .ics files or implement CalDAV endpoints.

Ensure line lengths do not exceed 75 octets and follow folding rules (leading space or tab on continuation lines).

By internalizing RFC 5545’s object model and syntax rules, you’ll be able to serialize and deserialize calendars across any platform.

